WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:06.640 --> 00:12.150
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

00:12.150 --> 00:15.400
Good afternoon.

00:15.400 --> 00:18.640
Earlier today the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) approved new measures

00:18.640 --> 00:22.030
to support the recovery and employment growth.

00:22.030 --> 00:24.380
I'll get to the specifics of our actions in a few moments,

00:24.380 --> 00:27.010
but I'll first describe the economic conditions

00:27.010 --> 00:30.550
that motivated the Committee's decision to take additional actions.

00:30.550 --> 00:35.070
As you know, the Federal Reserve conducts monetary policy under a dual mandate

00:35.070 --> 00:38.830
from Congress to promote maximum employment and price stability.

00:38.830 --> 00:43.860
The United States has enjoyed broad price stability since the mid-1990s

00:43.860 --> 00:46.850
and continues to do so today.

00:46.850 --> 00:51.350
The employment situation, however, remains a grave concern.

00:51.350 --> 00:54.580
While the economy appears to be on a path of moderate recovery,

00:54.580 --> 00:55.860
it isn't growing fast enough

00:55.860 --> 00:59.880
to make significant progress reducing the unemployment rate.

00:59.880 --> 01:04.820
Fewer than half of the 8 million jobs lost in the recession have been restored.

01:04.820 --> 01:08.730
And, at 8.1 percent, the unemployment rate is nearly unchanged

01:08.730 --> 01:11.970
since the beginning of the year and is well above normal levels.

01:11.970 --> 01:16.690
The weak job market should concern every American.

01:16.690 --> 01:20.420
High unemployment imposes hardship on millions of people,

01:20.420 --> 01:25.150
and it entails a tremendous waste of human skills and talents.

01:25.150 --> 01:28.150
Five million Americans have been unemployed for more than six months,

01:28.150 --> 01:31.300
and millions more have left the labor force-many of them doubtless

01:31.300 --> 01:33.810
because they have given up on finding suitable work.

01:33.810 --> 01:38.400
As the skills of the long-term unemployed atrophy and as their connections

01:38.400 --> 01:41.730
to the labor market wither, they may find it increasingly difficult

01:41.730 --> 01:45.400
to get good jobs, to their and their families' cost, of course,

01:45.400 --> 01:48.590
but also to the detriment of our nation's productive potential.

01:48.590 --> 01:52.890
To help bolster the recovery and promote price stability,

01:52.890 --> 01:55.210
the FOMC has provided unprecedented levels

01:55.210 --> 01:58.670
of policy accommodation in recent years.

01:58.670 --> 02:01.490
With our main policy interest rate near its effective lower bound,

02:01.490 --> 02:04.160
we have been using two complementary tools to carry

02:04.160 --> 02:07.220
out monetary policy-balance sheet actions

02:07.220 --> 02:11.410
and forward guidance regarding how long we anticipate maintaining exceptional

02:11.410 --> 02:14.830
levels of policy accommodation.

02:14.830 --> 02:17.380
While providing this support, we have been prudent,

02:17.380 --> 02:21.560
carefully weighing the potential benefits and costs of each new policy action,

02:21.560 --> 02:25.960
and recognizing that monetary policy, particularly in the current circumstances,

02:25.960 --> 02:29.890
cannot cure all economic ills.

02:29.890 --> 02:32.260
The FOMC has taken several actions this year.

02:32.260 --> 02:35.090
In January, it extended its forward guidance,

02:35.090 --> 02:38.400
stating that it anticipated that the federal funds rate will remain near current

02:38.400 --> 02:42.370
levels until late 2014.

02:42.370 --> 02:44.600
In June, the Committee decided to continue through the end

02:44.600 --> 02:48.540
of the year the previously established program to extend the average maturity

02:48.540 --> 02:51.900
of the securities it holds by buying longer-term securities

02:51.900 --> 02:56.420
and selling an equivalent amount of shorter-term securities.

02:56.420 --> 03:00.290
However, incoming data confirm that the modest pace of growth continues

03:00.290 --> 03:04.940
to be inadequate to generate much progress on unemployment.

03:04.940 --> 03:08.160
With inflation anticipated to run at or below our 2 percent objective,

03:08.160 --> 03:10.160
the Committee has become convinced

03:10.160 --> 03:14.090
that further policy accommodation is warranted to strengthen the recovery

03:14.090 --> 03:19.800
and support the gains we have begun to see in housing and other sectors.

03:19.800 --> 03:22.730
Accordingly, the FOMC decided today on new actions,

03:22.730 --> 03:25.600
electing to expand its purchases of securities

03:25.600 --> 03:29.640
and extend its forward guidance regarding the federal funds rate.

03:29.640 --> 03:31.010
Specifically, the Committee decided

03:31.010 --> 03:33.880
to purchase additional agency mortgage-backed securities, or MBS,

03:33.880 --> 03:37.750
at a pace of $40 billion per month.

03:37.750 --> 03:42.950
The new MBS purchases-combined with the existing maturity extension program

03:42.950 --> 03:46.220
and the continued reinvestment of principal payments from agency debt

03:46.220 --> 03:51.180
and agency MBS already on our balance sheet-will result in an increase

03:51.180 --> 03:55.700
in our holdings of longer-term securities of about $85 billion each month

03:55.700 --> 03:57.290
for the remainder of the year.

03:57.290 --> 04:01.150
The program of MBS purchases should increase the downward pressure

04:01.150 --> 04:04.390
on long-term interest rates more generally, but also on mortgage rates,

04:04.390 --> 04:07.900
specifically, which should provide further support for the housing sector

04:07.900 --> 04:11.070
by encouraging home purchases and refinancing.

04:11.070 --> 04:15.740
The Committee also took two steps to underscore its commitment

04:15.740 --> 04:18.500
to ongoing support for the recovery.

04:18.500 --> 04:22.510
First, the Committee will closely monitor incoming information on economic

04:22.510 --> 04:24.960
and financial developments in coming months,

04:24.960 --> 04:27.940
and if we do not see substantial improvement in the outlook

04:27.940 --> 04:31.950
for the labor market, we will continue the MBS purchase program,

04:31.950 --> 04:34.430
undertake additional asset purchases,

04:34.430 --> 04:37.730
and employ our policy tools as appropriate until we do.

04:37.730 --> 04:41.780
We will be looking for the sort of broad-based growth in jobs

04:41.780 --> 04:44.980
and economic activity that generally signal sustained improvement

04:44.980 --> 04:48.930
in labor market conditions and declining unemployment.

04:48.930 --> 04:51.450
Of course, in determining the size, pace,

04:51.450 --> 04:55.470
and composition of any additional asset purchases, we will, as always,

04:55.470 --> 04:58.040
take appropriate account of the inflation outlook

04:58.040 --> 05:00.700
and of their efficacy and costs.

05:01.860 --> 05:03.390
Additionally, the Committee emphasized

05:03.390 --> 05:06.110
that it expects a highly accommodative stance of monetary policy

05:06.110 --> 05:08.590
to remain appropriate for a considerable time

05:08.590 --> 05:11.830
after the economic recovery strengthens.

05:11.830 --> 05:15.180
This should provide greater assurance to households and businesses

05:15.180 --> 05:20.420
that policy accommodation will remain even as the economy picks up.

05:20.420 --> 05:22.790
In particular, the Committee today kept the target range

05:22.790 --> 05:28.020
for the federal funds rate at 0 to ¼ percent and stated that it anticipates

05:28.020 --> 05:31.150
that exceptionally low levels for the federal funds rate are likely

05:31.150 --> 05:34.170
to be warranted at least through mid-2015.

05:34.170 --> 05:39.800
In conjunction with today's meeting, FOMC participants-the 7 Board members

05:39.800 --> 05:43.670
and 12 Reserve Bank presidents-submitted their individual economic projections

05:43.670 --> 05:50.130
and policy assessments for the years 2012 through 2015 and over the longer run.

05:50.130 --> 05:52.420
Committee participants' projections for the unemployment rate

05:52.420 --> 05:56.300
in the fourth quarter of this year have a central tendency of 8.0

05:56.300 --> 06:02.930
to 8.2 percent, declining to 6.0 to 6.8 percent in the fourth quarter of 2015,

06:02.930 --> 06:05.680
levels that remain somewhat above participants' estimates

06:05.680 --> 06:09.560
of the longer-run normal rate of unemployment.

06:09.560 --> 06:14.000
Participants' projections of inflation have a central tendency of 1.7 percent

06:14.000 --> 06:20.060
to 1.8 percent for this year and 1.8 percent to 2.0 percent for 2015.

06:20.060 --> 06:24.640
While the economy appears to be advancing at a moderate pace,

06:24.640 --> 06:27.990
with some improvements appearing in housing and elsewhere,

06:27.990 --> 06:32.480
FOMC participants see an economic outlook that remains uncertain.

06:32.480 --> 06:35.150
The economy continues to face economic headwinds,

06:35.150 --> 06:39.170
including the situation in Europe; tight credit for some borrowers;

06:39.170 --> 06:43.520
and fiscal contraction at the federal, state, and local levels.

06:43.520 --> 06:46.030
In addition, strains in global financial markets continue

06:46.030 --> 06:48.890
to pose significant downside risks.

06:50.050 --> 06:52.970
Before I take your questions, I'd like to briefly address three concerns

06:52.970 --> 06:57.070
that have been raised about the Federal Reserve's accommodative monetary policy.

06:57.070 --> 07:00.220
The first is the notion that the Federal Reserve's securities purchases are akin

07:00.220 --> 07:02.100
to fiscal spending.

07:02.100 --> 07:05.970
The second is that a policy of very low rates hurts savers.

07:05.970 --> 07:10.130
The third is that the Federal Reserve's policies risk inflation down the road.

07:10.130 --> 07:14.580
On the first concern, I want to emphasize that the Fed's purchases

07:14.580 --> 07:18.930
of longer-term securities are not comparable to government spending.

07:18.930 --> 07:23.480
The Federal Reserve buys financial assets, not goods and services.

07:23.480 --> 07:25.880
Ultimately, the Federal Reserve will normalize its balance sheet

07:25.880 --> 07:28.670
by selling these financial assets back into the market

07:28.670 --> 07:31.340
or by allowing them to mature.

07:31.340 --> 07:33.690
In the interim, the Federal Reserve's earnings from its holdings

07:33.690 --> 07:36.640
of securities are remitted to the Treasury.

07:36.640 --> 07:39.940
In fact, the odds are strong that the Fed's asset purchase programs,

07:39.940 --> 07:41.710
both through their net interest earnings

07:41.710 --> 07:44.170
and by strengthening the overall economy,

07:44.170 --> 07:47.890
will help reduce rather than increase the federal deficit and debt.

07:47.890 --> 07:52.670
On the second concern, my colleagues and I are very much aware that holders

07:52.670 --> 07:55.550
of interest-bearing assets, such as certificates of deposit,

07:55.550 --> 07:58.470
are receiving very low returns.

07:58.470 --> 08:01.340
But low interest rates also support the value of many other assets

08:01.340 --> 08:05.740
that Americans own, such as homes and businesses large and small.

08:05.740 --> 08:08.860
Indeed, in general, healthy investment returns cannot be sustained

08:08.860 --> 08:12.400
in a weak economy, and, of course, it is difficult to save for retirement

08:12.400 --> 08:16.250
or other goals without the income from a job.

08:16.250 --> 08:19.400
Thus, while low interest rates do impose some costs,

08:19.400 --> 08:23.520
Americans will ultimately benefit most from the healthy and growing economy

08:23.520 --> 08:25.460
that low interest rates help promote.

08:25.460 --> 08:31.430
And finally, on inflation: Inflation has varied in recent years with swings

08:31.430 --> 08:34.660
in global food and fuel prices caused by a range of factors,

08:34.660 --> 08:38.260
such as drought and geopolitical tensions.

08:38.260 --> 08:41.870
However, overall inflation has averaged very close to the Committee's goal

08:41.870 --> 08:45.300
of 2 percent per year for quite a few years now,

08:45.300 --> 08:46.600
and a variety of measures show

08:46.600 --> 08:50.380
that longer-term inflation expectations are quite stable.

08:50.380 --> 08:52.690
The Federal Reserve is fully committed to both sides

08:52.690 --> 08:55.780
of its mandate-to price stability as well

08:55.780 --> 09:00.070
as to maximum employment-and it has both the tools and the will to act

09:00.070 --> 09:04.700
at the appropriate time to avoid any emerging threat to price stability.

09:04.700 --> 09:05.060
Thank you.

09:05.060 --> 09:07.730
I'd be happy to respond to your questions.

09:07.730 --> 09:11.500
DARREN GERSH.

09:11.500 --> 09:14.970
Hi, Mr. Chairman, it's Darren Gersh, Nightly Business Report.

09:14.970 --> 09:18.310
Your forecast doesn't get back to full employment for four years,

09:18.310 --> 09:21.360
so could these new bond purchases go on for years?

09:21.360 --> 09:25.110
And can you give us a better idea of when you'll-if you have specifics in mind

09:25.110 --> 09:26.970
on when you'll know it's time to stop?

09:26.970 --> 09:27.730
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

09:27.730 --> 09:32.030
Yes. We'll be looking for signs that the economy is strong enough

09:32.030 --> 09:35.840
to promote improvement and sustained improvement in labor market conditions

09:35.840 --> 09:37.210
and declines in unemployment.

09:37.210 --> 09:40.340
I mean, that's-we're not going to be able to sustain purchases

09:40.340 --> 09:42.730
until we're all the way back to full employment, that's not the objective.

09:42.730 --> 09:44.720
The idea is to quicken the recovery,

09:44.720 --> 09:48.980
to help the economy begin to grow quickly enough to generate new jobs

09:48.980 --> 09:50.320
and reduce the unemployment rate.

09:50.320 --> 09:53.860
So that's the criterion we're looking at.

09:58.490 --> 09:59.580
KRISTINA PETERSON.

09:59.580 --> 10:01.690
Kristina Peterson of Dow Jones.

10:01.690 --> 10:05.410
The statement indicated that the highly accommodative stance would be maintained

10:05.410 --> 10:07.650
until after the recovery starts to strengthen,

10:07.650 --> 10:11.830
but there aren't any specific economic conditions that are described.

10:11.830 --> 10:13.120
Could you describe what those would be?

10:13.120 --> 10:16.680
Or is the Fed-the Fed seems reluctant to have done that so far.

10:16.680 --> 10:18.090
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

10:18.090 --> 10:21.660
Well, we've been talking about our communications at the FOMC

10:21.660 --> 10:25.040
and trying to think about how best to communicate to the public, you know,

10:25.040 --> 10:29.500
what our policy reaction function, so to speak, is.

10:29.500 --> 10:32.950
And we haven't, to this point, come to a set of numbers,

10:32.950 --> 10:35.220
a set of data that we can put out.

10:35.220 --> 10:39.430
But what we're trying to convey here is that we're not going to be premature

10:39.430 --> 10:41.420
in removing policy accommodation.

10:41.420 --> 10:44.600
Even after the economy starts to recover more quickly,

10:44.600 --> 10:48.050
even after the unemployment rate begins to move down more decisively,

10:48.050 --> 10:50.660
we're not going to rush to begin to tighten policy.

10:50.660 --> 10:54.500
We're going to give it some time to make sure the recovery is well established.

10:54.500 --> 10:56.060
STEVE LIESMAN.

10:59.460 --> 11:03.880
Mr. Chairman, I want to talk about that same line in the statement.

11:03.880 --> 11:09.140
Does that mean that your tolerance for inflation will be higher in coming years,

11:09.140 --> 11:10.860
in the middle of the recovery?

11:10.860 --> 11:14.780
And, if not, what good is that language there if it doesn't tell people

11:14.780 --> 11:17.740
that the reaction function relative to inflation has changed?

11:17.740 --> 11:22.540
Secondly, stock prices are up today, so are oil prices and gold.

11:22.540 --> 11:27.100
Why aren't those part of the same reaction to the Fed's acts today?

11:27.100 --> 11:28.100
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

11:28.100 --> 11:33.180
Well, our policy approach doesn't involve intentionally trying

11:33.180 --> 11:33.990
to raise inflation.

11:33.990 --> 11:34.950
That's not the objective.

11:34.950 --> 11:37.350
The idea is to make sure we provide enough support

11:37.350 --> 11:41.150
so the economy will grow fast enough to bring unemployment down over time.

11:41.150 --> 11:43.390
I mean, as we look back at the last six months or so,

11:43.390 --> 11:47.180
we've seen unemployment at basically the same place it was in January.

11:47.180 --> 11:51.230
We've seen not enough jobs growth to bring down the unemployment rate,

11:51.230 --> 11:54.380
and what we need to see is more progress.

11:54.380 --> 11:57.010
And that's what we will be looking at.

11:57.010 --> 11:59.190
In terms of the mid-2015 date,

11:59.190 --> 12:02.100
we think by that point that the economy will be recovering,

12:02.100 --> 12:04.590
we'll be providing the support it needs.

12:04.590 --> 12:08.470
But if you look at our projections, you'll see it doesn't involve any inflation,

12:08.470 --> 12:10.610
that we still believe that inflation is going

12:10.610 --> 12:12.460
to be close to our 2 percent target.

12:12.460 --> 12:13.630
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:13.630 --> 12:15.600
All right, I just need to follow up.

12:15.600 --> 12:19.830
Does this-so you're saying it does not include greater tolerance for inflation,

12:19.830 --> 12:23.310
that you will-you would reverse course if inflation were to be

12:23.310 --> 12:25.580
above your target level, even given that statement?

12:25.580 --> 12:25.980
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

12:25.980 --> 12:27.840
Well, if inflation goes above the target level,

12:27.840 --> 12:31.190
as we talked about in our statement in January, we take a balanced approach.

12:31.190 --> 12:34.000
We bring inflation back to the target over time,

12:34.000 --> 12:37.700
but we do it in a way that takes into account the deviations of both

12:37.700 --> 12:40.120
of our objectives, you know, from their targets.

12:40.120 --> 12:42.890
ZACHARY GOLDFARB.

12:44.510 --> 12:46.500
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

12:46.500 --> 12:49.570
Earlier this year, on two occasions, the Fed took policy actions,

12:49.570 --> 12:52.310
which you defended as extremely important for the economy, but,

12:52.310 --> 12:55.000
as you mentioned, there hasn't been any improvement in the labor markets

12:55.000 --> 12:56.470
since the beginning of the year.

12:56.470 --> 12:58.440
Why should people believe this will make a difference?

12:58.440 --> 13:02.330
And the projections seem to suggest it's approximately a 0.4 reduction

13:02.330 --> 13:03.390
in unemployment.

13:03.390 --> 13:07.050
Is that the limit of what Fed policies can do going forward?

13:07.050 --> 13:08.360
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

13:08.360 --> 13:10.740
Well, our assessment-I talked about this at my remarks

13:10.740 --> 13:13.850
at Jackson Hole-our assessment, and that of the research literature,

13:13.850 --> 13:17.890
is that the policies that we've undertaken have had real benefits

13:17.890 --> 13:21.300
for the economy-that they have provided some support,

13:21.300 --> 13:26.050
that they have eased financial conditions and help reduce unemployment.

13:26.050 --> 13:30.560
All that being said, monetary policy, as I've said many times, is not a panacea.

13:30.560 --> 13:33.480
It's not by itself able to solve these problems.

13:33.480 --> 13:37.900
We're looking for policymakers in other areas to do their part.

13:37.900 --> 13:41.290
We'll do our part, and we'll try to make sure that unemployment moves

13:41.290 --> 13:44.100
in the right direction, but we can't solve this problem by ourselves.

13:44.100 --> 13:44.490
ZACHARY GOLDFARB.

13:44.490 --> 13:46.550
And do you think that 0.4 percent difference

13:46.550 --> 13:50.060
in the projections is about what's possible?

13:50.060 --> 13:50.590
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

13:50.590 --> 13:51.920
Well, what happens is going to depend

13:51.920 --> 13:57.360
on where the economy goes-how much ultimate accommodation we give the economy.

13:57.360 --> 14:00.880
The 0.4 percent you're referring to is the change in the forecast

14:00.880 --> 14:03.320
between the last projection and this one.

14:03.320 --> 14:08.000
But remember, people make projections assuming that policy is appropriate.

14:08.000 --> 14:11.470
So some of them may have assumed these policies in their last projections,

14:11.470 --> 14:13.950
and not all are assuming these policies in this projection.

14:13.950 --> 14:16.080
So that's probably a little bit of an understatement

14:16.080 --> 14:17.670
of what we think we can get.

14:17.670 --> 14:20.190
But in any case, again, I want to be clear

14:20.190 --> 14:23.080
that while I think we can make a meaningful and significant contribution

14:23.080 --> 14:28.310
to this problem-to reducing this problem, we can't solve it.

14:28.310 --> 14:32.320
We don't have tools that are strong enough to solve the unemployment problem.

14:32.320 --> 14:37.970
MIKE MCKEE.

14:37.970 --> 14:41.570
You've made an eloquent explanation over the past couple of weeks

14:41.570 --> 14:44.870
of the Fed's ability to lower interest rates.

14:44.870 --> 14:48.920
But what's missing for many economists is how the transmission mechanism is

14:48.920 --> 14:51.070
going to work.

14:51.070 --> 14:53.790
Most people think this will have a minimal effect on rates.

14:53.790 --> 14:57.340
Can you give us an idea of how much you think it might push rates down,

14:57.340 --> 15:01.970
and why moving rates down a few basis points might change demand,

15:01.970 --> 15:03.760
which seems to be the problem in the economy?

15:03.760 --> 15:05.010
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

15:05.010 --> 15:07.270
Well, the ultimate effect is going to depend, of course,

15:07.270 --> 15:09.460
on how much we end up doing, and that, in turn,

15:09.460 --> 15:11.180
is going to depend on what the economy does.

15:11.180 --> 15:12.960
This is a conditional program;

15:12.960 --> 15:17.150
we're going to be providing accommodation according to how the economy evolves.

15:17.150 --> 15:19.650
I think that's the virtue of putting it this way,

15:19.650 --> 15:22.300
is that if the economy is weaker, we'll provide more support;

15:22.300 --> 15:26.850
if the economy strengthens on its own or other headwinds die down,

15:26.850 --> 15:28.280
then it will require less support.

15:28.280 --> 15:30.880
So the amount of support we provide is going to depend

15:30.880 --> 15:33.280
on how the economy evolves.

15:33.280 --> 15:38.950
We do think that these policies can bring interest rates down-not just Treasury

15:38.950 --> 15:41.620
rates, but a whole range of rates, including mortgage rates

15:41.620 --> 15:47.610
and rates for corporate bonds and other types of important interest rates.

15:47.610 --> 15:49.010
It also affects stock prices.

15:49.010 --> 15:51.910
It affects other asset prices-home prices, for example.

15:51.910 --> 15:55.110
So looking at all the different channels of effect,

15:55.110 --> 15:57.880
we think it does have impact on the economy.

15:57.880 --> 16:02.140
It will have impact on the labor market, but, as again,

16:02.140 --> 16:05.800
the way I would describe it is a meaningful effect, a significant effect,

16:05.800 --> 16:10.690
but not a panacea, not a solution for the whole issue.

16:10.690 --> 16:13.300
We're just trying to get the economy moving in the right direction,

16:13.300 --> 16:16.820
to make sure that we don't stagnate at high levels of unemployment,

16:16.820 --> 16:25.270
that we're making progress towards more acceptable levels of unemployment.

16:25.270 --> 16:26.960
ROBIN HARDING.

16:26.960 --> 16:28.960
Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

16:28.960 --> 16:32.130
Mr. Chairman, is this the limit of what the Fed could do?

16:32.130 --> 16:35.150
You refer in your statement to other policy tools.

16:35.150 --> 16:38.000
If the unemployment situation doesn't improve,

16:38.000 --> 16:40.250
then what other measures do you have available?

16:40.250 --> 16:41.150
Thank you.

16:41.150 --> 16:41.950
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

16:41.950 --> 16:43.750
Well, there's a variety of possibilities,

16:43.750 --> 16:45.800
and we continue to look at all different options.

16:45.800 --> 16:48.890
But the two primary types of tools, as I've discussed,

16:48.890 --> 16:51.760
are balance sheet actions-and, of course, we can restructure those,

16:51.760 --> 16:56.490
change those in various ways; the other type of tool is communication tools.

16:56.490 --> 17:00.690
And we could-we continue to work on how best to communicate with the public

17:00.690 --> 17:02.630
and how best to assure the public

17:02.630 --> 17:07.040
that the Fed will remain accommodative long enough to ensure recovery.

17:07.040 --> 17:09.200
So, working with our communications tools,

17:09.200 --> 17:12.200
clarifying our response to economic conditions,

17:12.200 --> 17:17.000
might be one way in which we could further provide accommodation.

17:21.150 --> 17:22.430
PEDRO DA COSTA.

17:22.430 --> 17:23.850
Pedro da Costa from Reuters.

17:23.850 --> 17:27.140
My question is-I want to go back to the transmission mechanism because,

17:27.140 --> 17:30.190
speaking to people on the sidelines of the Jackson Hole conference,

17:30.190 --> 17:33.210
that seemed to be the concern about the remarks that you made is

17:33.210 --> 17:37.060
that they could clearly see the effect on rates and they could see the effect

17:37.060 --> 17:40.650
on the stock market, but they couldn't see how that had helped the economy.

17:40.650 --> 17:42.900
So I think there's a fear that, over time,

17:42.900 --> 17:45.010
this has been a policy that's helping Wall Street

17:45.010 --> 17:46.840
but not doing that much for Main Street.

17:46.840 --> 17:51.580
So could you describe, in some detail, how does it really different-differ

17:51.580 --> 17:53.300
from trickle-down economics,

17:53.300 --> 17:56.250
where you just pump money into the banks and hope that they lend?

17:56.250 --> 17:57.380
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

17:57.380 --> 18:01.460
Well we are-this is a Main Street policy,

18:01.460 --> 18:04.100
because what we're about here is trying to get jobs going.

18:04.100 --> 18:05.660
We are trying to create more employment,

18:05.660 --> 18:09.780
we are trying to meet our maximum employment mandate, so that's the objective.

18:09.780 --> 18:14.920
Our tools involve-I mean, the tools we have involve affecting financial asset

18:14.920 --> 18:18.540
prices, and that's-those are the tools of monetary policy.

18:18.540 --> 18:23.860
There are a number of different channels-mortgage rates,

18:23.860 --> 18:26.400
I mentioned other interest rates, corporate bond rates,

18:26.400 --> 18:30.840
but also the prices of various assets, like, for example, the prices of homes.

18:30.840 --> 18:36.270
To the extent that home prices begin to rise, consumers will feel wealthier,

18:36.270 --> 18:39.130
they'll feel more disposed to spend.

18:39.130 --> 18:43.300
If house prices are rising, people may be more willing to buy homes

18:43.300 --> 18:45.100
because they think that they'll, you know,

18:45.100 --> 18:47.700
make a better return on that purchase.

18:47.700 --> 18:50.750
So house prices is one vehicle.

18:50.750 --> 18:54.600
Stock prices-many people own stocks directly or indirectly.

18:54.600 --> 18:59.190
The issue here is whether or not improving asset prices generally will make

18:59.190 --> 19:00.940
people more willing to spend.

19:00.940 --> 19:04.560
One of the main concerns that firms have is there is not enough demand,

19:04.560 --> 19:07.450
there's not enough people coming and demanding their products.

19:07.450 --> 19:10.120
And if people feel that their financial situation is better

19:10.120 --> 19:12.510
because their 401(k) looks better or for whatever reason,

19:12.510 --> 19:15.230
their house is worth more, they are more willing to go out and spend,

19:15.230 --> 19:18.070
and that's going to provide the demand that firms need in order

19:18.070 --> 19:21.030
to be willing to hire and to invest.

19:22.300 --> 19:24.160
JON HILSENRATH.

19:25.770 --> 19:27.950
Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

19:27.950 --> 19:31.750
Mr. Chairman, the statement says-we've come back to this a couple of times-

19:31.750 --> 19:34.660
"If the outlook for the labor market does not improve substantially,

19:34.660 --> 19:37.190
the Committee will continue its purchases

19:37.190 --> 19:39.090
of agency mortgage-backed securities, ...

19:39.090 --> 19:40.880
additional asset purchases, and employ ...

19:40.880 --> 19:42.280
other policy tools."

19:42.280 --> 19:45.720
Can you define and describe more specifically what

19:45.720 --> 19:47.820
"improve substantially" means?

19:47.820 --> 19:51.000
And what do you-what is the Committee referring to when it says

19:51.000 --> 19:52.740
"additional asset purchases" and "other ...

19:52.740 --> 19:54.120
tools." CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

19:54.120 --> 19:59.100
Well, again, we're looking for ongoing sustained improvement

19:59.100 --> 19:59.770
in the labor market.

19:59.770 --> 20:03.840
There's not a specific number we have in mind.

20:03.840 --> 20:06.970
But what we've seen in the last six months isn't it.

20:06.970 --> 20:12.100
We're looking for something that involves unemployment coming

20:12.100 --> 20:15.790
down in a sustained way, not necessarily a rapid way,

20:15.790 --> 20:18.440
because I don't know if our tools are that strong,

20:18.440 --> 20:22.480
but we'd like to see an economy which is strong enough

20:22.480 --> 20:26.890
that it will support improving labor market conditions

20:26.890 --> 20:29.180
and unemployment that's declining gradually over time.

20:29.180 --> 20:31.940
That's essentially what we're looking for.

20:31.940 --> 20:33.710
In terms of the tools that we have,

20:33.710 --> 20:37.160
we have the mortgage-backed security purchases, which we can continue or expand

20:37.160 --> 20:39.790
or change in any-you know, in various ways.

20:39.790 --> 20:44.710
We could also purchase, of course, Treasuries; we've retained that capacity.

20:44.710 --> 20:48.090
And in terms of other policies, again, there are a number of possibilities,

20:48.090 --> 20:52.940
but the one I mentioned to Zach in particular is our communication policies,

20:52.940 --> 20:56.900
finding ways to better explain our rate policies

20:56.900 --> 21:02.130
that will engender more-accommodative financial conditions.

21:02.130 --> 21:05.960
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

21:05.960 --> 21:07.920
Mr. Chairman, it seems pretty clear

21:07.920 --> 21:11.020
that the Fed's announcement today has created a good deal of confusion

21:11.020 --> 21:13.440
about how long you'll keep buying assets,

21:13.440 --> 21:16.160
judging from the questions in this room and outside of it.

21:16.160 --> 21:19.680
Why did you choose not to adopt a specific target?

21:19.680 --> 21:22.430
Did the Committee consider specific targets-there have been a number

21:22.430 --> 21:27.120
of proposals-and why did you choose not to do that?

21:27.120 --> 21:28.620
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

21:28.620 --> 21:32.470
Well, we-the problem is that, for this purpose,

21:32.470 --> 21:37.320
that what we're looking for is a general improvement in labor market conditions.

21:37.320 --> 21:40.780
We want to see the unemployment rate come down,

21:40.780 --> 21:45.650
but that's not the only indicator, obviously, of labor market conditions.

21:45.650 --> 21:48.560
The unemployment rate came down last month because participation fell.

21:48.560 --> 21:51.510
That's not necessarily a sign of improvement.

21:51.510 --> 21:52.840
So we want to see more jobs.

21:52.840 --> 21:54.010
We want to see lower unemployment.

21:54.010 --> 21:57.640
We want to see a stronger economy that can cause the improvement

21:57.640 --> 22:01.850
to be sustained; it's not just a one-month or two-month phenomenon.

22:01.850 --> 22:04.740
We're not going to be looking for little wiggles in the numbers that are going

22:04.740 --> 22:07.200
to cause us to radically shift our policy.

22:07.200 --> 22:12.180
So, we, at least at this point, have decided to define it qualitatively.

22:12.180 --> 22:14.970
I hope I am giving you at least a little color in terms

22:14.970 --> 22:15.980
of what we'll be looking for.

22:15.980 --> 22:20.950
We'll be looking for, again, an economy which is quickening,

22:20.950 --> 22:25.950
that gives signs of continued improvement,

22:25.950 --> 22:30.770
that allows labor markets to be stronger,

22:30.770 --> 22:35.710
and that will be the type of qualitative criteria that we look at.

22:35.710 --> 22:40.050
We don't-again, we don't have a single number that captures that,

22:40.050 --> 22:44.160
but we anticipate that we'll have to do more, and we'll do enough to make sure

22:44.160 --> 22:48.930
that the economy gets on the right track.

22:48.930 --> 22:52.790
CRAIG TORRES.

22:52.790 --> 22:55.030
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

22:55.030 --> 22:59.120
Someone told me that less than 1 percent of all mortgages originated

22:59.120 --> 23:03.510
in the past 18 months went to borrowers with impaired credit history.

23:03.510 --> 23:06.640
So when we talk about people and a Main Street policy,

23:06.640 --> 23:09.580
it seems like you're struggling, like many other central banks,

23:09.580 --> 23:14.060
and that's to get the low rates down to-the challenge is to get them to people

23:14.060 --> 23:16.280
who really need them, people who are paying high rates

23:16.280 --> 23:20.810
or companies with somewhat fragile balance sheets.

23:20.810 --> 23:25.120
So, given that's the case-I mean, you guys got involved in markets

23:25.120 --> 23:27.200
when they were dysfunctional in the crisis.

23:27.200 --> 23:32.230
What's your appetite for doing more-targeted credit programs if, post-election,

23:32.230 --> 23:36.080
you had a Treasury Secretary and a Congress that was willing

23:36.080 --> 23:39.150
to underwrite some of the credit risk?

23:39.150 --> 23:39.920
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

23:39.920 --> 23:43.660
Well, now you're talking about congressional programs, and I don't, you know,

23:43.660 --> 23:45.280
I don't advocate specific programs.

23:45.280 --> 23:48.600
It's up to them to make those decisions.

23:48.600 --> 23:51.640
I think we're seeing modest improvement in mortgage markets.

23:51.640 --> 23:54.460
One thing that's helping is a stronger housing market.

23:54.460 --> 23:58.350
One reason that lenders have been very constrained is they are worried

23:58.350 --> 24:00.090
about further house price declines

24:00.090 --> 24:04.670
that will make the collateral worth less than the loan.

24:04.670 --> 24:09.630
As house prices have begun to rise, as the economy has gotten a little stronger,

24:09.630 --> 24:14.130
lending standards have eased just a bit.

24:14.130 --> 24:16.280
There's also been other changes which are useful.

24:16.280 --> 24:19.450
I note, for example, that the FHFA

24:19.450 --> 24:23.900
and the GSEs have recently changed their policy on putbacks

24:23.900 --> 24:26.750
so that banks will have more certainty

24:26.750 --> 24:31.500
about under what conditions a mortgage will be put back to them if it defaults.

24:31.500 --> 24:33.050
So I think there's number of things in train

24:33.050 --> 24:37.560
that will make the mortgage markets a little bit more open,

24:37.560 --> 24:40.420
and that is one factor, actually,

24:40.420 --> 24:43.350
that could make our policy more effective rather than less effective

24:43.350 --> 24:47.160
over time-if more people have access to mortgage credit,

24:47.160 --> 24:50.430
more people can take advantage of the low rates that we're providing.

24:53.400 --> 24:57.520
GREG IP. Greg Ip of the Economist.

24:57.520 --> 25:00.170
Mr. Chairman, one of the innovations of your statement today is that you have

25:00.170 --> 25:03.640
for the first time explicitly predicated your monetary policy action

25:03.640 --> 25:05.930
on the achievement of explicit economic goals,

25:05.930 --> 25:08.450
in this case a substantial improvement in the labor market.

25:08.450 --> 25:10.670
Could you give us some explanation of how

25:10.670 --> 25:15.190
that conditioning will make your policy more effective

25:15.190 --> 25:17.610
than if you had simply done as you previously have:

25:17.610 --> 25:19.970
announced the policy and then conducted it?

25:19.970 --> 25:23.290
And a technical question: When "Operation Twist" ends,

25:23.290 --> 25:27.460
do you anticipate adjusting the size of your asset purchases in order

25:27.460 --> 25:31.760
to maintain the $85 billion monthly flow of long-term asset purchases?

25:31.760 --> 25:33.350
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

25:33.350 --> 25:38.410
On the latter, when Operation Twist ends, we will be looking at the whole set

25:38.410 --> 25:41.460
of asset purchases in order to make decisions.

25:41.460 --> 25:45.090
We'll be looking at the state of the economy, as we described in the statement.

25:45.090 --> 25:47.280
In particular, what's the state of the labor market,

25:47.280 --> 25:51.230
what's the state of the outlook for economic growth?

25:51.230 --> 25:55.340
On conditioning, our policies have always been conditional

25:55.340 --> 25:58.940
in that we've always been clear that our asset purchases, for example,

25:58.940 --> 26:02.340
were reviewed periodically to see if they were still necessary,

26:02.340 --> 26:04.210
if they needed to be expanded.

26:04.210 --> 26:07.230
We did extend the maturity extension program, for example,

26:07.230 --> 26:09.780
when we thought that more support was needed for the economy,

26:09.780 --> 26:13.770
so our policies have always had a significant element of conditionality.

26:13.770 --> 26:18.920
But the idea here is to make that more explicit, more transparent to the public,

26:18.920 --> 26:22.340
make it more obvious that the Fed will do what's needed

26:22.340 --> 26:25.160
to provide the support for the economy.

26:25.160 --> 26:29.400
And we hope that what that will do is provide a bit more assurance,

26:29.400 --> 26:35.210
maybe a bit more confidence, that the Fed will be there to do what it can.

26:35.210 --> 26:40.960
Again, we're not promising, you know, a cure to all these ills.

26:40.960 --> 26:42.970
But what we can do is provide some support,

26:42.970 --> 26:48.320
and by assuring the public that we will be prepared to take action

26:48.320 --> 26:52.800
if the economy falters, we're hopeful that that will increase confidence

26:52.800 --> 27:02.160
and make people more willing to invest, hire, and spend.

27:02.160 --> 27:05.020
PETER BARNES.

27:05.020 --> 27:07.490
Sir, just to follow up on-Peter Barnes with Fox Business-to follow

27:07.490 --> 27:11.410
up on Darren's questions, a question about getting back to full employment.

27:11.410 --> 27:14.530
It looks like there's a lot more work to do here.

27:14.530 --> 27:17.470
And so I wanted to ask you about your plans

27:17.470 --> 27:21.990
as Fed Chairman-your term expires in January of 2014.

27:21.990 --> 27:26.130
Governor Romney's comments notwithstanding, what are your plans?

27:26.130 --> 27:27.970
Do you plan to leave at that time?

27:27.970 --> 27:33.380
Would you consider an appointment to a third term at the Fed at all?

27:33.380 --> 27:35.900
And then, if I may, on election year politics,

27:35.900 --> 27:39.030
is there any-do you have any concern or was there any discussion

27:39.030 --> 27:44.140
within the Committee about whether or not your actions today might be perceived

27:44.140 --> 27:46.680
as helping President Obama, helping the economy,

27:46.680 --> 27:49.610
and thus helping President Obama get reelected

27:49.610 --> 27:53.690
and hurting Governor Romney's chances in the presidential contest?

27:53.690 --> 27:54.190
Thank you.

27:54.190 --> 27:54.580
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

27:54.580 --> 27:57.260
Well, on the former, I'm-I have a lot to do.

27:57.260 --> 28:02.680
I am very focused on my work, and I don't have any decision or any information

28:02.680 --> 28:05.500
to give you on my personal plans.

28:05.500 --> 28:08.670
On the politics, we have tried very,

28:08.670 --> 28:12.100
very hard-and I think we've been successful-at the Federal Reserve

28:12.100 --> 28:13.950
to be nonpartisan and apolitical.

28:13.950 --> 28:20.860
We make our decisions based entirely on the state of the economy and the needs

28:20.860 --> 28:23.530
of the economy for policy accommodation.

28:23.530 --> 28:26.640
So we just don't take those factors into account,

28:26.640 --> 28:30.160
and we think that's the best way to maintain our independence

28:30.160 --> 28:37.080
and maintain the trust of the public.

28:41.010 --> 28:41.880
DONNA BORAK.

28:41.880 --> 28:44.160
Chairman, Donna Borak with American Banker.

28:44.160 --> 28:46.990
My question pertains to Basel III.

28:46.990 --> 28:50.010
Community bankers, as you know, have been very worried about the impact

28:50.010 --> 28:51.710
that these rules will have on their banks,

28:51.710 --> 28:55.400
especially given the fact that there's been some industry consolidation,

28:55.400 --> 28:58.920
and some have even questioned whether or not the Fed has actually looked

28:58.920 --> 29:02.760
at the impact that the rules would have on smaller-sized institutions.

29:02.760 --> 29:07.940
So my question for you is, will there be relief for the smaller institutions,

29:07.940 --> 29:10.010
and can you provide any assurance

29:10.010 --> 29:13.360
that this will not be a one-size-fits-all regulation?

29:13.360 --> 29:13.930
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

29:13.930 --> 29:14.530
Certainly.

29:14.530 --> 29:18.440
We are very interested in and very focused on community banks at the Fed.

29:18.440 --> 29:20.770
We believe they play a very important role

29:20.770 --> 29:23.440
in our economy and in our communities.

29:23.440 --> 29:29.040
We have a number of ways of communicating with community banks.

29:29.040 --> 29:33.780
It includes our advisory council made up of community bankers.

29:33.780 --> 29:37.980
It includes a special set of programs we have to reach out

29:37.980 --> 29:39.310
and talk to community bankers.

29:39.310 --> 29:41.170
So we are very interested in their views.

29:41.170 --> 29:45.260
I speak regularly to conventions and the like and talk to various groups.

29:45.260 --> 29:53.310
In terms of Basel III, of course, it's not one-size-fits-all.

29:53.310 --> 29:56.890
Many-and, indeed, many of the most difficult,

29:56.890 --> 29:59.310
complex regulations apply only to the largest

29:59.310 --> 30:02.410
and most complex institutions-for example,

30:02.410 --> 30:05.910
the capital surcharge that the largest banks have to hold,

30:05.910 --> 30:11.330
the complex rules applying to trading books and derivatives,

30:11.330 --> 30:17.300
the extra supervision under section 165, the orderly liquidation authority,

30:17.300 --> 30:22.110
the liquidity rules-the whole range of things that apply only to the largest,

30:22.110 --> 30:24.100
most complex, and internationally active banks.

30:24.100 --> 30:28.660
For the smaller banks, what our proposed rule does is try

30:28.660 --> 30:30.710
to strengthen their capital,

30:30.710 --> 30:34.780
and many small banks will already meet those capital requirements.

30:34.780 --> 30:37.290
Smalls banks tend to be very well capitalized.

30:37.290 --> 30:39.550
But, of course, it's important for small banks

30:39.550 --> 30:41.780
to be well capitalized as well as large banks.

30:41.780 --> 30:43.230
And there's a leverage requirement.

30:43.230 --> 30:47.620
But, again, most of the rules, most of the-particularly the most complex rules

30:47.620 --> 30:50.330
in Basel III will not apply to the smaller banks.

30:50.330 --> 30:55.160
Indeed, banks under $500 million have special exemptions from these rules.

30:55.160 --> 31:00.750
Having said all of that, I remind you that what we have now is a proposed rule,

31:00.750 --> 31:02.370
and we're receiving comment on that.

31:02.370 --> 31:06.260
We have a subcommittee of our supervision committee

31:06.260 --> 31:10.750
with two experienced-one community banker, one community bank supervisor on it

31:10.750 --> 31:14.190
from our Board who are particularly interested in making sure

31:14.190 --> 31:16.550
that the rules are not excessively onerous,

31:16.550 --> 31:21.320
and we will be looking at the comments and trying to make sure that we take

31:21.320 --> 31:25.350
into account the needs of community banks when we put out the final rule.

31:25.350 --> 31:28.900
GREG ROBB.

31:33.090 --> 31:37.430
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

31:37.430 --> 31:40.860
Yesterday former Fed Governor Larry Meyer at a conference

31:40.860 --> 31:45.140
in Washington said he'd never seen such a divided Fed.

31:45.140 --> 31:48.810
And we see it, we who cover the Fed see it in the speeches,

31:48.810 --> 31:50.770
in the run-up to today's decision.

31:50.770 --> 31:54.870
Some people said that it was dubious whether QE3 would work.

31:54.870 --> 31:58.170
Could you comment on former Governor Meyer's suggestions,

31:58.170 --> 32:00.440
and then-and sometimes don't you think,

32:00.440 --> 32:02.490
don't you wish that some of the Fed officials

32:02.490 --> 32:08.000
who don't support QE would keep their-keep their fears to themselves?

32:08.000 --> 32:09.170
Thank you.

32:09.170 --> 32:09.770
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

32:09.770 --> 32:14.010
Well, as you know, we are living in a very complex time

32:14.010 --> 32:19.510
and dealing with a complex economic situation and a variety of novel

32:19.510 --> 32:24.670
and different issues, including new policies that haven't been used

32:24.670 --> 32:28.870
in the same way in the past, and so naturally we have a range

32:28.870 --> 32:30.730
of views, a range of opinions.

32:30.730 --> 32:35.680
I think on the whole that's probably a good thing.

32:35.680 --> 32:38.200
It's good to hear different points of view,

32:38.200 --> 32:40.160
and it's good to make sure that the points of view

32:40.160 --> 32:43.220
that are outside the Fed are reflected in the discussion

32:43.220 --> 32:45.300
around the table inside the Fed.

32:45.300 --> 32:49.590
So we have a very collaborative and collegial discussion process, that,

32:49.590 --> 32:53.440
again-that spans a range of views.

32:53.440 --> 32:57.360
We were, however, able to come to a pretty good consensus-as you know,

32:57.360 --> 33:03.950
the vote on this was 11 to 1-and that's a sign that the broad center

33:03.950 --> 33:08.070
of the Committee does support these actions and will continue

33:08.070 --> 33:09.750
to support them going forward.

33:09.750 --> 33:12.080
GREG ROBB.

33:12.080 --> 33:16.680
Does the negative commentary hurt QE?

33:16.680 --> 33:21.250
Could it, if people in the market don't think it will work?

33:21.250 --> 33:23.230
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

33:23.230 --> 33:28.210
There's going to be negative commentary whether it comes

33:28.210 --> 33:31.040
from Fed officials or not.

33:31.040 --> 33:32.260
Again, because there's a range

33:32.260 --> 33:35.380
of views-some people think it's more effective than others.

33:35.380 --> 33:40.050
I discussed some of the evidence in my speech in Jackson Hole,

33:40.050 --> 33:43.060
and I talked about the fact that, you know,

33:43.060 --> 33:46.930
different researchers have gotten different estimates of the impact.

33:46.930 --> 33:50.400
Virtually all of them find that there is some beneficial impact,

33:50.400 --> 33:52.370
but they disagree on how much.

33:52.370 --> 33:54.300
So, there's going to be disagreement.

33:54.300 --> 33:57.760
And again, I personally don't think that it's a panacea,

33:57.760 --> 34:01.000
I personally don't think it's going to solve the problem.

34:01.000 --> 34:03.680
But I do think it has enough force

34:03.680 --> 34:07.830
to help nudge the economy in the right direction.

34:07.830 --> 34:13.900
MICHELLE FLEURY.

34:13.900 --> 34:17.760
You said that you can't cure all ills, that you haven't got strong enough tools

34:17.760 --> 34:19.700
to deal with the unemployment problem.

34:19.700 --> 34:22.170
I was curious to know what policy actions you'd

34:22.170 --> 34:24.950
like to see outside the Fed to try and address this.

34:24.950 --> 34:28.100
And, secondly, also on the "fiscal cliff," the expected spending cuts

34:28.100 --> 34:31.980
and tax increases, how concerned are you about that?

34:31.980 --> 34:35.440
And what ammunition do you have to deal with that, if that becomes a problem?

34:35.440 --> 34:37.960
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

34:37.960 --> 34:42.970
Well there's, again, a range of areas where actions could be taken,

34:42.970 --> 34:45.980
and I can't really prescribe all those possible responses.

34:45.980 --> 34:47.720
I would focus, I think, on the fiscal side.

34:47.720 --> 34:51.230
We currently have the so-called fiscal cliff.

34:51.230 --> 34:55.780
If no action is taken, there's going to be a very substantial increase in taxes

34:55.780 --> 35:00.400
and cut in spending on January 1 of the coming year.

35:00.400 --> 35:04.110
The CBO has suggested that if that's allowed to take place,

35:04.110 --> 35:07.090
that it would cause unemployment to begin to rise,

35:07.090 --> 35:09.750
and it might throw the economy back into recession.

35:09.750 --> 35:12.830
So I think one very basic thing that could be done

35:12.830 --> 35:16.910
to help address the recovery-the weakness of the recovery

35:16.910 --> 35:19.790
and the need for more employment-would be

35:19.790 --> 35:24.540
to address the fiscal cliff while simultaneously addressing longer-term fiscal

35:24.540 --> 35:27.480
sustainability issues which remain, of course, very serious.

35:27.480 --> 35:30.910
So that's one area where there is a lot of potential benefit.

35:30.910 --> 35:35.440
If the fiscal cliff isn't addressed, as I've said,

35:35.440 --> 35:39.600
I don't think our tools are strong enough to offset the effects

35:39.600 --> 35:44.270
of a major fiscal shock, so we'd have to think

35:44.270 --> 35:47.170
about what to do in that contingency.

35:47.170 --> 35:51.140
So I think it's really important for the fiscal policymakers to, you know,

35:51.140 --> 35:53.490
work together and try to find a solution for that.

35:53.490 --> 35:59.670
PATRICK WELTER.

35:59.670 --> 36:01.390
Mr. Chairman, my name is Patrick Welter

36:01.390 --> 36:04.160
with the German newspaper Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung.

36:04.160 --> 36:06.010
I have two questions, if I may.

36:06.010 --> 36:07.970
One is in the projections.

36:07.970 --> 36:10.530
In the economic and inflation projections,

36:10.530 --> 36:13.810
you foresee a low inflation rate below 2 percent

36:13.810 --> 36:18.090
to 2015-not you personally, but the FOMC.

36:18.090 --> 36:21.140
I am wondering, if you look at the growth rates,

36:21.140 --> 36:27.580
you have growth rates of about 3.4 percentage points in 2014 and 2015.

36:27.580 --> 36:30.520
How long do you think that it might work that you have

36:30.520 --> 36:34.140
such strong growth and no inflation pressure?

36:34.140 --> 36:36.900
And the second question, if I may, is,

36:36.900 --> 36:43.650
a lot of economists don't see too much effect out of the further round of QE3.

36:43.650 --> 36:48.180
Aren't you worried that, in promising that you will do whatever you can,

36:48.180 --> 36:52.010
even if it is tiny, small, that you give some kind of carte blanche

36:52.010 --> 36:56.240
to the fiscal policy and to the Congress not to do enough

36:56.240 --> 37:01.590
on their side of the policy action?

37:01.590 --> 37:02.610
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

37:02.610 --> 37:07.460
Well, the-on inflation, we do anticipate at some point what's normal

37:07.460 --> 37:11.920
in a recovery, which is, given that the economy fell very quickly

37:11.920 --> 37:15.970
and there's a lot of unused capacity, there's a lot of slack in the economy,

37:15.970 --> 37:17.520
it would be normal that there would be a period

37:17.520 --> 37:21.030
where the economy would grow faster than trend in order to make up some

37:21.030 --> 37:23.960
of the slack that was created.

37:23.960 --> 37:29.630
So we don't anticipate the economy is going to be overheating anytime soon.

37:29.630 --> 37:36.060
And as long as we pay close attention to inflation expectations as well

37:36.060 --> 37:38.440
as the trajectory of the economy,

37:38.440 --> 37:43.020
we think inflation will remain close to our 2 percent target.

37:43.020 --> 37:47.460
On your second question, certainly,

37:47.460 --> 37:50.940
there is a range of views on how effective these tools are.

37:50.940 --> 37:56.220
I've spent a lot of time, as all of my colleagues have, looking at the evidence,

37:56.220 --> 38:00.500
and, of course, the staff here have done a great deal of work on the question.

38:00.500 --> 38:03.550
And the bottom line for most of it, most of the research,

38:03.550 --> 38:09.900
is that while these tools are not so powerful that they can solve the problem,

38:09.900 --> 38:16.500
they are at least able to provide meaningful support to the economy.

38:16.500 --> 38:20.780
Our job is to use the tools we have to meet our mandate,

38:20.780 --> 38:23.470
which is maximum employment and price stability.

38:23.470 --> 38:27.640
So if we have tools that we think can provide some assistance

38:27.640 --> 38:29.150
and we're not meeting our mandate,

38:29.150 --> 38:32.750
then I think that our obligation is to do what we can.

38:32.750 --> 38:36.940
Of course, we would like to see policies across the board

38:36.940 --> 38:39.370
to help address these issues.

38:39.370 --> 38:45.730
But, you know, that's not our province; we are the monetary policy authorities,

38:45.730 --> 38:52.740
and our job is to use monetary policy as effectively as we can.

38:55.480 --> 38:57.630
STEVE BECKNER.

38:57.630 --> 39:01.050
Steve Beckner of MNI, Mr. Chairman.

39:01.050 --> 39:05.120
There have been concerns raised,

39:05.120 --> 39:09.540
questions raised by people like Columbia Professor Michael Woodford

39:09.540 --> 39:14.050
and others about the credibility of your forward guidance on the path,

39:14.050 --> 39:19.720
the future path of the federal funds rate-the idea being that,

39:19.720 --> 39:23.190
to the extent it's conditional, it's not really convincing

39:23.190 --> 39:27.450
and doesn't provide the kind of confidence that you referred to.

39:27.450 --> 39:32.810
Now, on this latest statement, you've removed some of that conditionality.

39:32.810 --> 39:35.900
I am particularly struck by the statement that "the Committee expects

39:35.900 --> 39:40.690
that a highly accommodative stance of monetary policy will remain appropriate

39:40.690 --> 39:44.930
for a considerable time after the economic recovery strengthens."

39:44.930 --> 39:49.800
I assume that was done to make your forward guidance more credible,

39:49.800 --> 39:55.680
and yet the question remains whether, you know, as the economy picks up steam,

39:55.680 --> 39:59.180
whether the FOMC will really follow through and keep rates low

39:59.180 --> 40:02.580
or whether you will do as the Fed has always done

40:02.580 --> 40:05.720
and begin to raise the funds rate.

40:05.720 --> 40:06.280
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

40:06.280 --> 40:08.630
Well, that's an important question.

40:08.630 --> 40:13.960
Michael Woodford- who, by the way, is my former colleague and coauthor

40:13.960 --> 40:19.030
and friend, so I know him quite well, and I know his works quite well-I think,

40:19.030 --> 40:23.390
actually, the thrust of his research is that forward guidance-communication

40:23.390 --> 40:27.860
about future policy-is, in fact, the most powerful tool that central banks have

40:27.860 --> 40:29.880
when the interest rate is close to zero.

40:29.880 --> 40:34.360
And he advocates policies like nominal GDP targeting, for example,

40:34.360 --> 40:39.550
that would essentially require credibility lasting many years,

40:39.550 --> 40:45.310
the implication being that the Fed would target the nominal level of GDP

40:45.310 --> 40:48.730
and promise to do that for many years in the future even if inflation, you know,

40:48.730 --> 40:51.010
rose as part of that policy.

40:51.010 --> 40:59.180
So his own perspective is that credibility is the key tool

40:59.180 --> 41:04.930
that central banks have in order to get traction at the zero lower bound.

41:04.930 --> 41:09.360
Whether we have the credibility to persuade markets that we'll follow

41:09.360 --> 41:11.490
through is an empirical question.

41:11.490 --> 41:15.120
And the evidence, which I also, again, discussed in my remarks recently,

41:15.120 --> 41:17.970
is that when we've announced extended guidance,

41:17.970 --> 41:20.410
that financial markets have responded to that,

41:20.410 --> 41:24.950
that private-sector forecasters have changed their estimates

41:24.950 --> 41:27.070
of what unemployment and inflation will be

41:27.070 --> 41:29.680
when the Fed begins to remove accommodation.

41:29.680 --> 41:31.870
So the empirical evidence is

41:31.870 --> 41:38.390
that our announcements do have considerable credibility.

41:38.390 --> 41:40.180
And I think there's a good reason for that,

41:40.180 --> 41:46.160
which is that we have talked a lot both publicly and privately

41:46.160 --> 41:50.570
about the rationale for maintaining rates low even as the economy strengthens,

41:50.570 --> 41:57.120
and I think the basic ideas are broadly espoused within the Committee.

41:57.120 --> 42:01.260
And so there is a consensus that even as the personnel change

42:01.260 --> 42:04.580
and so on going forward, that this is the appropriate approach,

42:04.580 --> 42:10.020
and that by following through, we will have created a reserve of credibility

42:10.020 --> 42:14.350
that we can use in any subsequent episodes that occur.

42:14.350 --> 42:23.550
DON LEE. Don Lee with the L.A. Times.

42:23.550 --> 42:27.280
With mortgage rates already at historical lows,

42:27.280 --> 42:31.050
how much further do you think the actions, your actions,

42:31.050 --> 42:34.360
will drive down the rates, and related to that,

42:34.360 --> 42:38.310
I'm assuming that you expect the purchases of mortgage-backed securities

42:38.310 --> 42:44.390
to have a meaningful effect on refinancings and housing activity.

42:44.390 --> 42:46.210
What would that look like?

42:46.210 --> 42:47.630
What would that meaningful effect mean?

42:47.630 --> 42:49.390
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

42:49.390 --> 42:53.370
Well, again, as I mentioned before,

42:53.370 --> 42:56.670
it's true that our mortgage-backed securities purchases ought to drive

42:56.670 --> 42:59.600
down mortgage rates, and put downward pressure on mortgage rates,

42:59.600 --> 43:04.830
and create more demand for homes and more refinancing.

43:04.830 --> 43:08.430
But it will depend, again, ultimately on several things.

43:08.430 --> 43:11.970
One will be on the amount that we do, the amount of purchases that we do,

43:11.970 --> 43:15.990
and that in turn is going to be a function of how the economy evolves.

43:15.990 --> 43:19.960
If the economy is weaker, we'll do more, and in those cases,

43:19.960 --> 43:21.800
probably rates would be pretty low at any case

43:21.800 --> 43:23.960
because the economy is looking weak.

43:23.960 --> 43:29.220
If the economy is stronger, strong enough

43:29.220 --> 43:33.130
to create improving labor market conditions, we won't have to do as much.

43:33.130 --> 43:36.400
And so, the amount that we do depends on how the economy evolves.

43:36.400 --> 43:39.310
So, since I don't know exactly how much we'll end up doing,

43:39.310 --> 43:43.690
it's a little bit hard to give you an exact estimate.

43:43.690 --> 43:50.990
So I think that's, you know, in terms of how many homes and those kinds

43:50.990 --> 43:52.680
of questions, again, I think

43:52.680 --> 43:56.050
that the markets are looking-are looking a little better.

43:56.050 --> 44:00.380
I think that house prices are beginning to rise in some markets,

44:00.380 --> 44:02.390
which will encourage people to look at homes,

44:02.390 --> 44:05.160
will encourage lenders to make more mortgage loans.

44:05.160 --> 44:07.670
So I'm hopeful that we'll see continued progress in the housing market;

44:07.670 --> 44:12.810
that-that has been one of the missing pistons in the engine here.

44:12.810 --> 44:15.270
Housing is usually a big part of the recovery process.

44:15.270 --> 44:18.210
We haven't had that nearly to the usual extent,

44:18.210 --> 44:20.330
and to the extent that we can support housing,

44:20.330 --> 44:24.840
I think that would be a very useful outcome.

44:24.840 --> 44:29.950
DON LEE. There doesn't seem to be that many people

44:29.950 --> 44:33.260
who could qualify for refinancings.

44:33.260 --> 44:39.260
Can we expect a meaningful effect on an increase in refinancing activity?

44:39.260 --> 44:39.650
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

44:39.650 --> 44:44.340
Well, I think there'll be some, but you get more benefit when people buy homes.

44:44.340 --> 44:49.360
And sales of homes are down still, but they have been rising steadily,

44:49.360 --> 44:54.400
and we're trying to provide more support for people who want to go out

44:54.400 --> 44:59.120
and buy homes, construct homes, and also those who want to refinance.

44:59.120 --> 45:02.990
But it's the purchases of new homes that generate the construction activity,

45:02.990 --> 45:06.730
the furnishing, all those things that help the economy grow.

45:08.390 --> 45:12.830
SCOTT SPOERRY.

45:12.830 --> 45:16.710
Scott Spoerry with CNN and CNNMoney.

45:16.710 --> 45:19.530
Earlier this year at one of your news conferences in this room,

45:19.530 --> 45:23.370
you said that you were already hearing anecdotal information from some

45:23.370 --> 45:27.670
of your colleagues at the regional Fed Banks about firms,

45:27.670 --> 45:33.000
companies making decisions on hiring next year because they were afraid

45:33.000 --> 45:36.140
of the fiscal cliff or whatever the federal government was going to do

45:36.140 --> 45:38.710
in terms of-in terms of cutbacks.

45:38.710 --> 45:42.240
It's been a few months since you made that statement,

45:42.240 --> 45:45.920
and I'm sure your staff are working hard on it,

45:45.920 --> 45:53.330
but how much of a headwind to the economy is the fear

45:53.330 --> 45:58.380
of the federal government just sort of cutting way back,

45:58.380 --> 46:02.030
falling across the fiscal cliff or even-even if they take a few steps back

46:02.030 --> 46:04.160
from the cliff, it's still there.

46:04.160 --> 46:07.970
How much is that fear contributing to lack of-lack of growth

46:07.970 --> 46:11.140
or adequate GDP growth in the economy?

46:11.140 --> 46:12.030
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:12.030 --> 46:15.620
Well I-you know, it's pretty hard to give you a number,

46:15.620 --> 46:21.680
but I can certainly confirm that as the Reserve Bank presidents

46:21.680 --> 46:26.580
and Governors made their reports today and yesterday around the table,

46:26.580 --> 46:31.460
there was considerable discussion of uncertainty, including policy uncertainty,

46:31.460 --> 46:35.450
fiscal policy uncertainty, and the implications of that

46:35.450 --> 46:38.030
for hiring and investment decisions.

46:38.030 --> 46:43.540
A lot of-a lot of firms are waiting to see whether

46:43.540 --> 46:46.590
that problem will be resolved.

46:46.590 --> 46:48.480
And if so, how?

46:48.480 --> 46:52.250
And I think it is a concern.

46:52.250 --> 46:56.460
It is something that is affecting behavior now.

46:56.460 --> 46:59.040
But again, I don't know-I don't have a number,

46:59.040 --> 47:02.970
I don't know how big that effect is, but, certainly,

47:02.970 --> 47:05.640
the sooner that can be resolved, the sooner it can be clarified,

47:05.640 --> 47:09.890
it will be beneficial not just because we avoid the cliff itself,

47:09.890 --> 47:17.000
but because we clarify for firms, for employers and investors,

47:17.000 --> 47:18.700
how that's going to be resolved.

47:18.700 --> 47:24.870
So, I think it's an issue that is of some consequence, yes.

47:24.870 --> 47:26.050
CATHERINE HOLLANDER.

47:26.050 --> 47:29.920
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

47:29.920 --> 47:31.800
Catherine Hollander, National Journal.

47:31.800 --> 47:35.420
How much was the fiscal cliff a decision, or a factor in your decision,

47:35.420 --> 47:40.210
to do an open-ended QE instead of a fixed sum?

47:40.210 --> 47:41.730
Or fiscal uncertainty more generally?

47:41.730 --> 47:42.450
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

47:42.450 --> 47:44.900
Well, we take the economy as we find it.

47:44.900 --> 47:49.540
There are a lot of headwinds right now that are affecting the economy.

47:49.540 --> 47:51.420
There's fiscal headwinds.

47:51.420 --> 47:56.570
There's international factors, including the situation in Europe.

47:56.570 --> 48:02.460
There's factors arising from still-impaired credit markets, and so on.

48:02.460 --> 48:07.810
So we looked at that-looked at the economy from the perspective of, you know,

48:07.810 --> 48:12.910
how quickly it's been growing over the last six months to a year.

48:12.910 --> 48:15.670
And, as I talked about in a speech in March,

48:15.670 --> 48:22.140
in order for employment gains to be sustained, for unemployment to fall,

48:22.140 --> 48:26.020
the economy needs to grow at or above trend levels.

48:26.020 --> 48:29.140
And lately it's not really been at trend.

48:29.140 --> 48:34.840
So we've been responding to that problem and trying to take steps

48:34.840 --> 48:39.030
that will assure somewhat stronger growth and, we hope,

48:39.030 --> 48:42.030
will help bring unemployment down over time.

48:42.030 --> 48:45.640
Now, again, the fiscal cliff, the uncertainty about the fiscal cliff,

48:45.640 --> 48:49.470
is one of the factors, one of the headwinds, but I'm sure there are many others,

48:49.470 --> 48:53.120
and we don't try to differentiate among them in any sense.

48:53.120 --> 48:58.000
If the fiscal cliff does occur, I suspect it won't, and I hope it won't,

48:58.000 --> 49:01.700
but if it does, and we get the kind

49:01.700 --> 49:05.150
of impact the Congressional Budget Office is talking about, as I've said,

49:05.150 --> 49:08.660
I don't think the Federal Reserve has the tools to offset that,

49:08.660 --> 49:12.500
and we would have to rethink at that point.

49:12.500 --> 49:15.190
But we've taken the steps we've taken now because we'd

49:15.190 --> 49:18.680
like to see the economy gather more momentum, and the more momentum it has,

49:18.680 --> 49:22.960
the better placed we are to deal with any shocks that might come down the road.

49:24.840 --> 49:30.670
MARCY GORDON.

49:30.670 --> 49:32.760
Marcy Gordon with the Associated Press.

49:32.760 --> 49:36.920
One of the aspects we've seen in recent reports

49:36.920 --> 49:40.800
on unemployment is the shrinking labor force.

49:40.800 --> 49:44.190
Is that something that's of specific concern to you,

49:44.190 --> 49:48.040
and what does it tell us about the labor market and the economy?

49:48.040 --> 49:49.740
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

49:49.740 --> 49:50.840
Well, you are absolutely right.

49:50.840 --> 49:56.830
And as I mentioned earlier, the unemployment decline last month was more

49:56.830 --> 50:00.710
than 100 percent accounted for by declines in participation.

50:00.710 --> 50:05.710
Some decline in participation is anticipated, is as expected.

50:05.710 --> 50:07.060
We're an aging society.

50:07.060 --> 50:10.600
We have more people retiring.

50:10.600 --> 50:14.670
Female participation has flattened out;

50:14.670 --> 50:18.730
it hasn't continued to climb as it did for several decades.

50:18.730 --> 50:22.460
We're seeing less participation among younger people,

50:22.460 --> 50:25.030
fewer college students taking part-time jobs and the like.

50:25.030 --> 50:28.320
So part of this decline in participation was something

50:28.320 --> 50:33.780
that we anticipated quite a long time ago, but part of it is cyclical.

50:33.780 --> 50:35.440
Part of it reflects the fact

50:35.440 --> 50:39.930
that some people-because they have essentially given up

50:39.930 --> 50:44.580
or at least are very discouraged-have decided to leave the labor force.

50:44.580 --> 50:48.140
And the anticipation is that if the economy really were to strengthen,

50:48.140 --> 50:49.470
and labor markets were to strengthen,

50:49.470 --> 50:52.360
at least some of those people would come back into the labor force.

50:52.360 --> 50:55.170
They might even temporarily raise the unemployment rate

50:55.170 --> 50:58.220
because they're now looking again.

50:58.220 --> 51:02.860
So the participation rate over and above-the decline in participation rate over

51:02.860 --> 51:06.590
and above the downward trend is just one of the other indicators

51:06.590 --> 51:08.020
of a generally weak labor market.

51:08.020 --> 51:13.690
And it's why I said earlier that we do want to look at a range of indicators,

51:13.690 --> 51:17.810
not just the unemployment rate, although that's a very important indicator,

51:17.810 --> 51:24.600
not just payrolls, although that also is a leading indicator, but participation,

51:24.600 --> 51:29.110
hours, part-time work, and a variety of other measures which suggest

51:29.110 --> 51:34.510
that our labor market is still in quite weak condition.

51:34.510 --> 51:35.650
Thank you.

